A||||4||SLYGUY||Limp pedal.....||||8||
Z||000000||SLYGUY||10-16-2001||03:48 AM||||Here goes:<P>The car wasn't easy to get into first gear from the get go. Once the car warmed up, it got a little easier.<P>Tonight, I had to have the clutch buried into the carpet to get the car into first. <BR>I figured that I needed to bleed the lines. <BR>I found the bleed valve. It had a cap that refused to come off. Had to rip it off.<BR>Had an assistant push the clutch pedal to the floor. I cracked the bleeder counter clockwise and some fluid squirted out.<BR>Good so far right...<BR>Then closed the valve careful not to turn it too far. Had the assistant pump the clutch pedal (is this right?) and hold it to the floor. Opened the valved, less fluid squirted out. Checked the level in the master cyclinder and it had dropped a little. Topped it off. <P>Now the clutch pedal is a lifeless useless item. What did I do wrong?<BR>I think I remember hearing something about having the rear of the car on jackstands. I also read about it after I searched for "Bleeding Clutch".<BR>Did having the car on all four wheels pump the system full of air?<BR>HELP!!!!<P>Sly||||reg||8
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-16-2001||07:49 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P>Did you bleed the clutch, the brakes or refill the cooling system.<P>I'd check the shift cables.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||10-16-2001||09:20 PM||||I've got the same problem.  Bleed the thing 10 times, no luck.  Seems there is some fluid up under the dash too(I have no carpet)...I am thinking a trip to the auto parts store is necessary for a new clutch master cylendar.<P>It kinda disengages the clutch ok, and pumps fluid ok-visually(though clear tube), but it never extends the piston fully.<P>Oh well...hopefully I'll find one tomorrow.  I want to take my car on a G-tech run!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Turkey on Rye||10-16-2001||10:22 PM||||Hello,<BR>my name is chris and i am new to this forum. I had the same problem with my clutch. I bleed it for about 5 hours and never got any pedal back. Turned out that it was the Slave cylinder that was bad. Allowing air to be sucked back into the line when the clutch was let up. Hope this will help. Good luck and email me if you need anything else.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||10-17-2001||08:26 AM||||i've been fighting with it too. for me a gravity bleed was the only thing that worked, and even that didn't work very well. it seems like if you force fluid through the system in any way (pushing with the pedal or sucking with a vaccume) air gets in while you bleed. i still need to check the stroke of the slave shaft as suggested.||||reg||1
